  "notes": "Oakland's live-funk architect, and the two research phrases that become numbers are \"funk samples augmented by live drums\" and mixing of \"surgical precision.\" The second is the unusual one: a forward snare and a controlled low-mid is `velocityTiers` with the accent band pushed up and the main band tightened, plus the percussion gain brought two dB nearer the kit than the g-funk parent leaves it. He is the keyboardist as well as the producer, so the harmony is flat-7 Clinton/Bootsy vamp material — `i–VII` at the top weight, sevenths half the extension mass — and the lead is organ rather than the parent's portamento worm. The bass is not his: he works with a live bassist and reinforces it for car playback, which is a low `followRootsProb`, a high glide and a register that stays deep while staying 19 semitones wide. ⚠ `modes` are authored, not inherited: his band is 84–96 and the parent's Death Row moods pin 90–100, so the slower Dangerous Crew records would have been unreachable. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates him high-medium — the role, the gear lineage and the mixing description are documented, the tempo band and the voicing detail are read from the catalog. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 47 to 45 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 46 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 2.",
